{
  "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
  "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Philip Morris USA Inc.",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHAPIRO: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice,\nand may it please the Court:\nWe are here today because the Oregon court\nfailed to follow this Court's directions on remand and\nbecause the ground it gave is not adequate to show a\nforfeiture of due process rights.\nThis -- this Court vacated after finding\nthat the Oregon Supreme Court applied the wrong\nconstitutional standard, and it remanded with directions\nto apply the standard that the Court laid out. But the\nOregon court didn't do that. It never even addressed\nthe constitutional issue. The Oregon court, of course,\nrefused to follow this Court's direction because it\nbelieved there were mistakes in another paragraph in our\ninstruction request dealing with what the court referred\nto as \"unrelated issues.\"\nBut that isn't what this Court mandated."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Shapiro, we are\ndealing with a State supreme court, and our bottom line\nalways reads \"for further proceedings not inconsistent\nwith this opinion.\" And it was my understanding that a\nState court can resolve a case on an alternate State law\nground, if there is such a ground in the case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shapiro",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHAPIRO: Well, what -- what the State argued there was that for 60 years the \"correct in all respects\" rule was in effect in Alabama, and they cited dozens of cases applying it. But this Court unanimously held that that approach was pointless severity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There were not 28 cases in the Alabama Supreme Court applying the rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice STEVENS",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could you just tell me, well, why was it an exercise in futility? That's what I don't quite understand."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robert S. Peck",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PECK: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This Court's constitutional mandate in this case is conditioned in several significant respects, and it invites the discretion and judgment of a State court that's applying it. First of all, it says that States have flexibility in coming up with a procedure to address this procedural due process issue. It also says that it has to be an appropriate case; there has to be a significant risk of juror confusion, and a request. There's no indication in the opinion that this Court intended to federalize the State procedure over how that request occurs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ROBERT S. PECK ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What I'm saying is that they went further then, and this is what gave this Court the authority to rule on that substantive issue. They said that that request was inconsistent with the Oregon statute. And they did so -- on page 48a of the petition, where they say: \"In Williams 1, the Court of Appeals concluded that the instruction was incorrect under State law. We agree.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What are the elements? Imagine -- I'm trying to get help, if I were to try to put pen to paper on this. Suppose they win in this. Then we will be back at the State law issue that I thought was going to be there, which was the issue of -- you are talking about the colloquy. Did they give the essence of the Federal mandated instruction, or didn't they?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would urge you, Justice Breyer, to look at the original case in 1916, the Sorenson case. There the court was face with a question: If there is the kernel of a correct instruction in there, is that adequate to ask the court to give that instruction or should we insist on what they thought at the time was the majority rule in the United States, that we should insist on an instruction that is clear and correct in all respects, and that the -- that the counsel has the responsibility to provide that? And they decided to go with the clear and correct rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This is page 37a of our joint appendix. The -- the court concludes the amount of punitive damages you assess may not exceed the sum of one hundred million dollars. And that, of course, was the zone of reasonableness that the jury inferred from this, suggesting a global punishment to the jury with no protection."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Which portion of the charge specifically are you referring to?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What about this point that was made that in 2002 that is exactly what Philip Morris did, give one simple, precise instruction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shapiro",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHAPIRO: Yes. This -- this State Farm instruction was broken out. That's an option for lawyers. But under the pattern instruction, it's quite proper to put them all in one instructional basket."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1216",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3c84f40bf6562a6989e5fb170bfec3c3473bd1df7ef7ec0ab1352a2b10747c02",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3c84f40bf656-0003-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 11:04 a.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
